use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: Popup Menus, part of the user interface series.
*/
class UserInterfacesPopupMenu is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/popupMenu.html")
        SetShortTitle("Popup Menus")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("How to make a Quorum popup menu, attach it to a control, open it from code or the keyboard, add a submenu, and make sure every command can be reached without a mouse.")

        Title("Popup Menus")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("When you right-click a word in a word processor, or select it and press
"):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(" or the Menu key on Windows, a small menu appears next to it, with commands like Cut, Copy and Paste. This is a
"):Strong("popup menu"):Text(", also called a "):Strong("context menu"):Text(", because its commands fit
the thing you are interacting with using the mouse or keyboard. Popup menus keep commands close to the content
they change, without a menu bar. In this tutorial, you will attach a popup
menu to a text field on a page, open one from a button, and add a submenu.
You will also learn the keys that open a popup menu, and
why a program should not hide a command only in a popup menu.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Create a "):Code("PopupMenu"):Text(" and fill it with "):Code("MenuItem"):Text(" commands")
        ListItem("Attach a popup menu to a control with "):Code("SetPopupMenu"):Text(", so right-clicking or "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(" opens it")
        ListItem("Open a popup menu from your own code with "):Code("OpenAt")
        ListItem("Explain how a keyboard or screen reader user reaches the commands in a popup menu")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Right-click (or "):Key("Ctrl"):Text("+click on a Mac) on three different things on your
computer: a file, a word in a document, and an empty part of the desktop.
On Windows, you can also select each one and press "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(" or the Menu
key instead. Write down which commands appear each time. Why are they different? Which
way is faster for you: the mouse or the keys?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/PopupMenu/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/userInterface/PopupMenu/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_TESTING_AND_REFINING_19)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_TESTING_AND_REFINING_19)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_USER_EXPERIENCE_4)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        PopupMenusAndMenuBars()
        ABehaviorThatChangesText()
        AttachingAPopupMenu()
        OpeningWithTheKeyboard()
        OpeningFromCode()
        Submenus()
    end

    private action PopupMenusAndMenuBars
        Subsection("Popup menus and menu bars")
        Paragraph("A popup menu is built from the same "):Code("MenuItem"):Text(" objects as a menu bar, which
you met in the "):Link("Menus", "/tutorials/ui/menu.html"):Text(" tutorial. The difference is
where it lives:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("a "):Strong("menu bar"):Text(" is always on screen, in a row of the page;")
        ListItem("a "):Strong("popup menu"):Text(" is hidden until something opens it, appears on top of
everything else, and closes as soon as a command is chosen, the user
clicks somewhere else, or the user presses "):Key("Esc"):Text(" (keys below).")

        Paragraph("You never add a popup menu to a page. You attach it to a control, and it
shows itself when it opens.")
    end

    private action ABehaviorThatChangesText
        Subsection("A behavior that changes text")
        Paragraph("The commands in this tutorial change the text in a text field, which you
met in the "):Link("Text Fields", "/tutorials/ui/textField.html"):Text(" tutorial. Add a new
class named "):Code("ChangeTextBehavior"):Text(". It remembers which text field to
change and what to do to it:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/ChangeTextBehavior.quorum")
    end

    private action AttachingAPopupMenu
        Subsection("Attaching a popup menu to a control")
        Paragraph("Now replace "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(". It adds a text field to the page, makes a popup
menu with three commands, then attaches the menu to the field with
"):Code("SetPopupMenu"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/Main.quorum")

        Paragraph("Run the program and right-click the text field, or press "):Key("Tab"):Text(" until the
field has the focus and press "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(". With a click, the menu opens
where you clicked; with "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(", it opens at the text cursor. Choose Uppercase, and the text changes. When the menu closes, the
focus goes back to the text field.")

        Paragraph("Notice that the text field's description tells the user that "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text("
opens the options. A screen reader reads the description, so a user who
cannot see the screen learns that the menu exists.")
    end

    private action OpeningWithTheKeyboard
        Subsection("Opening a popup menu with the keyboard")
        Paragraph("Many people open popup menus with the keyboard. People who don't use a
mouse need the keys, and many others, such as programmers and other
professionals who work mostly from the keyboard, use them because they are
faster than reaching for the mouse. These keys open the popup menu attached to a control, when that control has the
focus:")

        Table("Keys that open a popup menu")
        TableHeader():Cell("Control"):Cell("Keys that open its popup menu")
        TableRowWriter fieldRow = TableRow()
        fieldRow:Cell("Text field")
        fieldRow:Cell():Text("Right-click, or "):Keys("Shift", "F10")
        TableRowWriter boxRow = TableRow()
        boxRow:Cell("Text box")
        boxRow:Cell():Text("Right-click, "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(", or "):Key("Ctrl"):Text("+click on a Mac")
        TableRowWriter treeRow = TableRow()
        treeRow:Cell("Tree")
        treeRow:Cell():Text("Right-click, "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(", or "):Key("Ctrl"):Text("+click on a Mac")
        TableRowWriter buttonRow = TableRow()
        buttonRow:Cell("Button")
        buttonRow:Cell():Text("Right-click, or "):Key("Ctrl"):Text("+click on a Mac")

        Paragraph("With the mouse, the menu opens where you clicked. With "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(", it
opens at the text cursor.")

        Paragraph("Once a popup menu is open, it has the keyboard focus, but nothing in it is
chosen yet. Press "):Key("Down"):Text(" or "):Key("Up"):Text(" first, then use these keys:")

        Table("A popup menu's keys")
        TableHeader():Cell("Key"):Cell("What it does")
        TableRowWriter downRow = TableRow()
        downRow:Cell():Key("Down arrow")
        downRow:Cell("Chooses the first item or moves down one item.")
        TableRowWriter upRow = TableRow()
        upRow:Cell():Key("Up arrow")
        upRow:Cell("Chooses the last item or moves up one item.")
        TableRowWriter rightRow = TableRow()
        rightRow:Cell():Key("Right arrow")
        rightRow:Cell("Opens the submenu of the chosen item.")
        TableRowWriter leftRow = TableRow()
        leftRow:Cell():Key("Left arrow")
        leftRow:Cell("Closes the submenu and goes back to its parent item.")
        TableRowWriter enterRow = TableRow()
        enterRow:Cell():Key("Enter"):Text(" or "):Key("Space")
        enterRow:Cell("Runs the chosen command or opens the chosen submenu.")
        TableRowWriter escapeRow = TableRow()
        escapeRow:Cell():Key("Esc")
        escapeRow:Cell("Closes the submenu or closes the popup menu.")

        Paragraph("Check the Button row of the first table. A button has no key that opens
its popup menu. If you attach a popup menu to a button, anyone using the
keyboard cannot reach it. This is why a program should never put a command "):Strong("only"):Text("
in a popup menu. Next, you will fix this by opening the menu from your own
code.")
    end

    private action OpeningFromCode
        Subsection("Opening a popup menu from code")
        Paragraph("Any behavior can open a popup menu with "):Code("OpenAt(x, y)"):Text(". The numbers are a
"):Strong("global position"):Text(", measured from the corner of the window, and the menu
opens with its top-left corner there. A button's "):Code("GetGlobalX"):Text(" and
"):Code("GetGlobalY"):Text(" give the global position of its bottom-left corner, so the
menu opens just below the button. Make a class named "):Code("OpenMenuBehavior"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/OpenMenuBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("Then add a "):DoubleQuote():Text("Text options"):DoubleQuote():Text(" button to the page. Add
"):Code("use Libraries.Interface.Controls.Button"):Text(" to the top of "):Code("Main"):Text(", and put
these lines just after "):Code("field:SetPopupMenu(menu)"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/ButtonLines.quorum")

        Paragraph("Now the same menu opens three ways: right-clicking the field or the button,
pressing "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(" in the field, or pressing the button with a click,
"):Key("Space"):Text(" or "):Key("Enter"):Text(". Every user can reach every command. The page puts the
button in the "):Key("Tab"):Text(" order after the field, so a keyboard user finds it by
pressing "):Key("Tab"):Text(".")

        BlockEditorFile("ChangeTextBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/ChangeTextBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditorFile("OpenMenuBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/OpenMenuBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditor("Main.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/Main2.quorum", "", true, true)
    end

    private action Submenus
        Subsection("Submenus in a popup menu")
        Paragraph("A popup menu can hold submenus too, built just like the submenus in a
menu bar. Add the submenu to the popup menu first, then add its commands.
This version of the menu lines in "):Code("Main"):Text(" puts the two case commands in a
"):DoubleQuote():Text("Change case"):DoubleQuote():Text(" submenu:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/PopupMenu/SubmenuLines.quorum")

        Paragraph("Keep popup menus short. A long menu is slow to read with a screen reader
and hard to use on a small screen. Usually, three to seven commands is
plenty.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you add a popup menu to a program you've already written,
and check on your own that it works with the mouse and with the keyboard.
The goal is to choose commands that fit a control's context, and to give
everyone a clear way to reach them. Pick a control from one of your earlier programs, such
as a list or a text box, and write down three commands that would make
sense in its context menu. Then add a popup menu with those commands. Make
sure every command can also be reached without a mouse, with "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(" or
a button that calls "):Code("OpenAt"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("When it works, test it both ways, with the mouse and with the keyboard, using
this self-check. Write
yes or no for each line, and what you did to find out:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("I can reach the control with "):Key("Tab"):Text(", and open its menu with "):Keys("Shift", "F10"):Text(".")
        ListItem("I can choose every command with the arrow keys and "):Key("Enter"):Text(".")
        ListItem("After a command runs, the focus is back on the control.")
        ListItem("The control's description tells a person that it has a menu, and how to
open it.")
        ListItem("Every command in the menu can also be reached some other way, such as a
button or a menu bar.")

        Paragraph("For each "):DoubleQuote():Text("no"):DoubleQuote():Text(", change your program and write down what you changed and
why. Then run the whole self-check again.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Popup menus are fast for people who know they are there, with the mouse or
the keyboard, and nearly invisible to everyone else.
How does a user learn that a popup menu exists? Name two ways your program
can tell them.")

        Paragraph("The next tutorial covers "):Link("Toolbars", "/tutorials/ui/toolbar.html"):Text(", a row of
buttons for the commands people use most.")
    end
end
